# Fuzz: one walk of a value against its schema (3.8.5) gives the verdict a reference gives.
#
# Schemas are drawn from a grammar over what bote judges: a type or none, an enum of
# strings, minLength and maxLength, minimum, maximum and both exclusive bounds on integers,
# items, minItems and maxItems, properties, required, additionalProperties (false, true or a
# schema), minProperties and maxProperties, nested two deep. Values are drawn alike: keys
# spelled plainly and escaped, some named twice, strings of one to four characters in one
# to four bytes each, escapes and surrogate pairs among them. (3.8.6, from the 3.8.5 review)
# Strings holding bytes that are no UTF-8, counted as a client's decoder reads them; half
# the `properties` padded with nine names no value uses, so a key is decoded and looked up
# past eight names; a 300-byte name, and keys spelling it plainly, with an escape, and one
# byte short. (3.8.7) Type arrays, `const` and `enum` of any values, `uniqueItems`, the
# reference's equality its own, a string that is no text equal to nothing for `const` and
# `enum` and read as a client reads it for `uniqueItems`. (3.8.8) `prefixItems`, `contains`
# with `minContains` and `maxContains`, `propertyNames` by a length or an enum,
# `dependentRequired` and `dependentSchemas`. (3.8.9) `allOf`, `anyOf`, `oneOf`, `not` and
# `if` / `then` / `else`. (3.8.10) Up to three definitions in the root's `$defs`, and a `$ref`
# to one anywhere, written plainly, percent-encoded or under an escaped key; a definition
# names only those after it, so no reference leads back. (3.8.12) `patternProperties` of three
# of those patterns, `additionalProperties` beside them. (3.8.11) `multipleOf` of 2, 3 or 0.5,
# judged by scaled integers, and five patterns, each judged by hand. (3.8.13) `format` of date,
# ipv4 or int32, each judged by hand, strings in the pool that meet them. (3.8.14) A root
# `$dynamicAnchor: "r"` and a `$dynamicRef: "#r"`, plainly or escaped, where a part of the value
# is judged, so it never leads back. The reference judges bayan's parse of both, a `$ref` by the
# definition it names and a `$dynamicRef` by the root. Exit codes:
#
#   10   a value bote passes the reference refuses, or the reverse;
#   20   the rounds drew fewer than a thousand values passed or a thousand refused;
#   30   judging left bytes on the heap.
#
# A value naming a key twice is drawn and skipped: bayan refuses one, so it cannot be the
# reference for it, and bote's reading of such a value (its first occurrence where the schema
# names the key) is what its unit tests check.

